build-translations must also gate data/, which needs msgfmt unconditionally.

WHY THE BUILD FAILS WITHOUT THIS:

    Program msgfmt found: NO
    data/meson.build:6:27: ERROR: Program 'msgfmt' not found or not executable

-Dbuild-translations=false was already passed and did not help, because that
option guards ONE line at meson.build:47:

    if get_option('build-translations')
        subdir('po')
    endif
    subdir('data')            <-- unconditional

data/ is always entered, and its first statement is i18n.merge_file(), which
merges the po catalogues into freedesktop.org.xml and therefore requires
msgfmt. So the option names a thing it does not fully control: turning
translations off still demands the translation toolchain.

THERE IS NO gettext IN THIS SYSTEM -- not a stage-5 package and not in the
stage-4 sysroot -- which is also why sixteen other recipes disable NLS. glib's
own [undeclarable] entry states the general case: xgettext being absent is the
only reason a set with no gettext recipe gets through at all.

THE .in FILE IS ALREADY VALID XML, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE COPY SAFE. Checked,
not assumed: data/freedesktop.org.xml.in parses with ElementTree, has 1038
mime-type entries and contains no gettext placeholders and no @VARIABLE@
substitutions -- zero matches for `^\s*<_` or `@[A-Z_]+@`. Translation here is
ITS-based (its/shared-mime-info.its marks what is translatable), so
merge_file's only effect is ADDING xml:lang variants of the human-readable
<comment> text. Copying the input verbatim yields the same database with
English descriptions, which is where the rest of this system already is.

WHAT IS LOST IS COMMENT TEXT IN OTHER LANGUAGES -- the string a file manager
shows for "PNG image". The globs, magic bytes, subclasses and aliases that
xdg_mime_get_mime_type_from_file_name() actually reads are not translated and
are unaffected.

freedesktop_org_xml IS STILL ASSIGNED IN BOTH BRANCHES, and its only consumers
are in tests/meson.build, which -Dbuild-tests=false does not enter. The
assignment is kept so the variable means the same thing on either path rather
than being conditionally undefined.

Generated with `diff -u`, not written by hand.

--- a/data/meson.build
+++ b/data/meson.build
@@ -3,15 +3,25 @@
